## Reference block

Several prompts below say "paste the reference block". This is it — the shared
schema facts, kept in one place so they only need correcting once.

```
Every question is this shape:

  id, category, measure, subtype, prompt, answer, min, max, scale, unit,
  referenceYear?, source { title, url }, explanation

Categories (all live): population, history, geography, science, animals,
space, technology, movies, dinosaurs, games

Subtype → measure → allowed units:
  country, city      → population → people
  event              → history    → year
  length             → size       → metre, kilometre
  area               → size       → square-kilometre
  mass               → size       → kilogram, tonne
  count              → quantity   → count
  percentage         → quantity   → percent
  money              → quantity   → usd
  duration           → physics    → second, minute, hour, day, duration-year
  speed              → physics    → kph
  temperature        → physics    → celsius

Scale is "linear" or "log".

Hard rules:
- `year` is a calendar point and renders CE/BCE. A span of years is
  `duration-year` — so "5,730 years" never renders as "5,730 CE".
- Temperature must be linear. Log cannot represent negatives.
- Money carries `referenceYear` and is never re-priced to today.
- Use log when min→max spans two or more orders of magnitude.
- `answer` must sit inside `min`..`max`, and not at either edge.
- Every question needs a real, checkable `source` with a working URL.
```

---

## 1. Draft new questions

```
I'm writing questions for Give or Take, a guess-the-number quiz. The player
sees a prompt and slides to estimate a value; scoring is by proximity.

<paste the reference block>

Write <N> questions in the <category> category.

Constraints:
- Vary the subtype across the batch — don't give me twelve `count` questions.
- The answer should be genuinely estimable by a reasonably informed adult,
  not a trivia recall test and not a coin flip.
- min/max should be a plausible playing field, not a giveaway. If the answer
  is 400, don't set the range 380–420.
- The prompt must be one clear sentence and must state the unit implicitly or
  explicitly, so the player knows what they're estimating.
- `explanation` is one or two sentences of the interesting bit — why the
  number is what it is, or context that makes it stick. Not a restatement.
- Cite a source I can actually open and verify. No "various sources".

Output valid JSON only — an array of question objects. No prose, no markdown
fences.
```

## 2. Fact-check a question pack

```
Audit the question pack below for a guess-the-number quiz. Be adversarial —
I'd rather you flag a false positive than let a wrong number ship.

<paste the reference block>

For each question, check:
1. Is `answer` actually correct? Verify against the cited source. If the
   source doesn't support the number, say so explicitly.
2. Does the source URL resolve, and is it the primary/authoritative one?
3. Is the number time-sensitive? If it drifts year to year and has no
   `referenceYear`, flag it.
4. Is `answer` inside `min`..`max` and away from the edges?
5. Is `scale` right for the range span?
6. Does subtype/measure/unit match the table above?
7. Is the prompt ambiguous — could a fair reader estimate a different
   quantity than the one being scored?

Output a table: id | verdict (pass / fix / kill) | what's wrong | suggested
fix. Then list the ids you'd ship as-is.

<paste pack JSON>
```

## 3. Balance a daily set

```
Give or Take deals five questions per daily set. I want the set to feel varied
and fair rather than lopsided.

Use the real exported functions from `lib/game.ts` — `valueToPosition` and
`scoreGuess` — to compute this. Do not reimplement the scoring maths; import
it and call it.

For the candidate pool below:
1. Compute, for each question, whether the answer sits below or above the
   centre of its slider range.
2. Deal questions into sets of five aiming for a ~2 below / 3 above split
   (or the reverse), distributed by remainder across all sets rather than
   front-loading.
3. Then run a swap pass minimising repeated categories within any one set —
   ideally five distinct categories per set.
4. Report the idle score per set (what a player scores leaving every slider
   untouched). Flag any set that lands outside roughly 900–2000 of the
   available <questions × 1000>.

Show me the plan before writing any files.
```

## 4. Codex / agent task brief

```
Repo: Cambo2k20/give-or-take-quiz — React 19 + TypeScript + Vite, Supabase
backend, deployed to GitHub Pages at giveortakequiz.com.

Ground yourself before you start: run `git status` and `git log --oneline -3`
and work from what they say, not from anything a doc or transcript claims.
Docs in this repo go stale.

Task: <describe the task>

Project rules you must not break:
- Postgres is the source of truth for the category bank.
  `lib/questions.generated.ts` is a committed snapshot — never hand-edit it,
  regenerate with `npm run generate:questions`.
- `data/daily-sets.json` is the source of truth for the daily — that one goes
  the other direction.
- Never reintroduce a hardcoded 10,000 score ceiling. Derive it as
  `questions.length * 1000`.
- `daily` is not a client-side GameMode. It stays `dailyDate: string | null`.
- Don't touch BASE_PATH handling without checking the Pages path.
- This repo is public. Don't commit unpublished daily content as a "backup".

Done when `npm run lint`, `npm test` and `npm run build` all pass clean.

Show me the plan and the diff before you commit anything.
```

## 5. Category icon generation

```
Create a single icon for the <category> category of a mobile quiz app.

Style:
- Glowing cosmic / sci-fi. Luminous edges, deep space-dark background.
- The concept must live in the SHAPE — a silhouette that reads correctly in
  one colour at 48px. Lighting and glow are finish, never the idea. If the
  subject is only recognisable because of the glow, it has failed.
- Centred, symmetrical-ish, generous margin, no text, no lettering.
- Square, flat-on, no perspective tricks, no drop shadow onto a surface.
- Accent colour: <hex>. Everything else neutral so the accent carries the
  category identity.

Subject: <what the icon depicts>

It has to sit in a set of 28 siblings — match weight, margin and glow
intensity to the existing ones rather than being individually striking.
```

## 6. Background theme brief

```
Build an animated background theme for Give or Take called "<name>", evoking
<concept>. Two files: a registry entry and an artwork component, following the
pattern in `src/themes/` and the guide in `docs/ADDING_THEMES.md`.

Performance budget — this is a hard constraint, not a preference:
- Animate ONLY `transform` and `opacity`. Never `filter`, never
  `background-position`, never geometry.
- Max 2–3 animating layers. Zero layers animating a filter.
- No `backdrop-filter` over an animated background.
- `will-change` on animated parents only, never on children.
- Below 600px: cut blur radius and drop a layer.
- Must hold 60fps at 390×844 on a mid-range Android.

Design constraint: the concept must be legible from the shapes alone. A theme
that only reads as its concept because of colour and glow will fail QA — this
has happened before (see `design-qa.md`, Cinema Glow).

Contrast: question text sits on top of this. Nothing in the background may
compete with it.
```

## 7. Add your own

Copy this block:

```markdown
## <n>. <Name>

Use when: <one line — the trigger, so future-you can find it>
Last checked: <date>

​```
<prompt text>
​```
```
